cuxcvr -- Command

The file-transfer half of cu, run on the remote system
/etc/cuxcvr r file ...
/etc/cuxcvr s file ...

cuxcvr is not a command anybody types.  It is the program cu(1) types for you
on the machine at the far end of the line, to move a file between the two.

When you give cu the escape ~g, cu sends the line

    /etc/cuxcvr r name

down the serial port, so that the remote shell runs it, and then reads the
packets it sends back into a local file.  The escape ~p sends

    /etc/cuxcvr s name

instead, and cuxcvr on the remote system reads the packets cu sends and
writes them into a file there.  The letter is the direction as seen from the
remote end: r sends a file back to you, s receives one from you.

More than one file name may follow.  They are taken in turn, and the first
one that fails ends the transfer.

The Transfer
Both ends speak the same packet protocol, from the same source cu is built
from.  A transfer opens with an F packet carrying the base name of the file --
the directory part is dropped, so the file lands in the working directory of
whichever end is receiving -- and continues with numbered I packets of up to
NPKT data bytes each.  The receiver acknowledges each one, and asks for a
retransmission of any packet that arrives damaged or out of sequence.  A
second F packet ends the transfer.

cuxcvr talks over its own standard input and output, which the remote shell
has connected to the serial line.  It puts that terminal into raw mode with
echo and carriage-return mapping off for the duration, and restores the
settings it found before it exits -- including on the error paths, so a failed
transfer does not leave the far end of the line in raw mode.

Diagnostics
A failure is reported to the local cu, which prints it, rather than to the
remote terminal you cannot see:

    file name too long		the base name does not fit in one packet
    cannot open file		r: the file is not there or is unreadable
    cannot create file		s: the file could not be made

Exit status is the number of files that failed, and 1 for a usage error.

Notes
This page has no COHERENT Lexicon article behind it.  The Lexicon documents
cu and mentions /etc/cuxcvr in passing as the helper the ~g and ~p escapes
invoke, but never describes it, and this system installs it as a program of
its own.  The page was written here, from the source in base/cmd/cu; none
of Mark Williams Company's text is in it.

The protocol is cu's own and is not the one uucp or kermit speaks, so both
ends of a transfer must be COHERENT machines running this cu.

